From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ville Takio <ville+git@takio.fi>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 16:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: b43: AC-PHY: send and receive outside the softirq

On a BCM4708 (two Cortex-A9 cores, DMA without cache coherency), the AC
cores lost a fifth of their 5 GHz throughput on the NAPI and direct TX
paths: 316-347 Mbit/s from the AP to a two-stream phone on an RT-AC56U,
against 397-421 before the N/HT series. Three costs added up:

- the 128-byte TX header in the headroom: frames bridged from bgmac have
  less, and each one was reallocated and copied (28857 of 28857 in a
  two-second sample);
- A-MSDUs copied into one buffer: about 15 us to map one, against 9 us
  for one mapped in place;
- one core: b43's RX, TX status and most of the TX posting ran in the
  softirq of the core that takes the interrupt, while the other kept a
  quarter idle.

For the AC cores only:

- b43_tx_direct() is false: RX and TX status run in the interrupt
  thread, TX in tx_work, and the received frames and the TX statuses go
  to mac80211 once the thread has dropped wl->mutex (b43_rx_deliver());
- the TX header takes a descriptor of its own, as below core rev 30, and
  mac80211 is asked for no headroom;
- A-MSDUs stay on their frag_list (TX_FRAG_LIST), a descriptor for each
  subframe;
- frames are pulled from the TXQs in tx_work, as long as the ring has
  room, so that mac80211 builds A-MSDUs of the ones that wait;
- RX syncs the header first and then the frame's length, from buffers
  mapped DMA_FROM_DEVICE, three to a page pool page: the poison of
  b43_poison_rx_buffer() no longer reaches memory on these cores;
- no airtime fairness, whose airtime estimate per frame costs about a
  twentieth of the throughput.

RT-AC56U (BCM4352, ch 36/80, Nord 2 2x2 at -44 dBm, TCP -P4), alternated
with the module before the N/HT series: 398-401 Mbit/s down and 384-392
up, against 410-419 and 402-409. RT-AC66U B1 (BCM4360, server on the
router): 336-338 down and 213-215 up, against 347-351 and 203.

Signed-off-by: Ville Takio <ville+git@takio.fi>
---
